  { q: 'Can you clear a house before probate is granted?', a: 'Yes, in most cases. As executor, you can arrange a clearance before the Grant of Probate is issued. This is common when the property needs to be secured or sold. We recommend keeping a record of valuable items — and we provide a full inventory as standard.' },
  { q: 'Who is responsible for clearing a house after someone dies?', a: 'The executor named in the will is legally responsible for managing the estate, including clearing the property. If there is no will, the next of kin can apply for Letters of Administration. Executors can appoint a clearance company to act on their behalf.' },
  { q: 'How soon after a death should you clear the house?', a: 'There is no legal deadline, but it\'s advisable to secure the property and begin the clearance process within a few weeks. Insurance policies may require the property to be checked regularly. If the property is to be sold, clearance is needed before marketing.' },
